# Poll-fire needs a cooldown (run-60 board fault)

Words: [README.md — Words used](../../README.md#words-used-in-this-project).
Copy 1 (copy 2: [docs/GAME_DESIGN.md](../../docs/GAME_DESIGN.md) Input section).

Board testing on run 60 found that a physical tap of the fire button fired
2-3 bullets in `ASTEROID.HTML` and `MISSILE COMMAND` (1 bullet under heavy
frame load). Root cause traced RTL-side, confirmed **not** an event bug:
both titles poll the held-fire state every frame — required for
hold-to-repeat, which must keep working — and a physical (or keyboard) tap
stays "held" for ~40-80ms (I2C batch quantum + finger dwell), which
quantizes to 2-3 polls at 60fps. It scales with frame rate, so it gets
worse as sim speed increases. An RTL-side fix was rejected as too risky
for hold-repeat semantics — the fix has to be content-side, in the fire
handler.

`setTimeout` is off the table (a per-call allocation), and a `dt`-based
clock is a trap on this platform: there is no `performance.now`, so `dt`
is usually a hardcoded per-call constant, not measured wall time — which
makes a `state.t`-style cooldown a disguised call-counter that quietly
re-couples to whatever the true poll rate turns out to be. Use a real
integer frame counter instead.

**Fix: gate the fire handler with a frame-counter cooldown — rate-limit
only, never edge-gate, so hold-to-repeat still works.**

```javascript
// BAD — bare poll, no cooldown: a 40-80ms tap is 2-3 polls at 60fps
function playerShoot() {
  if (game.over || !game.active) return;
  projectiles.push(makeShot());
}

// GOOD — INVFAST.HTML's playerShoot() pattern: frames-based, no
// setTimeout, no allocation. N≈9 for ~150ms at 60fps.
let canShootAt = 0;
function playerShoot() {
  if (game.over || !game.active) return;
  if (frames < canShootAt) return;
  canShootAt = frames + 9;
  projectiles.push(makeShot());
}
```

`storage/INVFAST.HTML`'s `playerShoot()` is the reference pattern.
`storage/ASTEROID.HTML`'s `firePlayer()` and `storage/MISSILE.HTML`'s
`fire()` both carry this gate as of the run-60 fix (`MISSILE.HTML` keeps
its counter on the `state` object — that file's top-level IIFE scope is
already at the 16-locals-per-function-scope RTL wall, so no new top-level
`var` may be added there; see `functional_model/compiler.py`'s
`_note_local`).
